{
  "rules": [
    "Correct for severe underestimation of volatility: The model consistently forecasts a smooth, monotonic trend (linear drift) while true outcomes exhibit high-amplitude oscillations (spikes and sharp drops). Apply a volatility multiplier to the forecast range rather than just adjusting the mean.",
    "Implement aggressive mean reversion after price spikes: When history shows a sharp rise (e.g., >5 EUR in 3 days), the model fails to anticipate the subsequent correction. Reduce the forecast slope immediately following any upward spike to account for the observed pullback.",
    "Invert the lag bias in recovery phases: The model lags behind the true trend during recovery from lows (e.g., May 2023). If the last 5 days of history show a consistent upward trajectory, increase the forecast slope by 20-30% to capture the momentum the model misses.",
    "Discard sentiment signals when they are near-zero: In periods where sentiment history is flat (0.00), the model's static forecast is particularly inaccurate. Do not rely on sentiment as a trigger for direction changes in these cases; instead, rely purely on technical momentum indicators.",
    "Adjust for horizon-specific decay: The model's error grows significantly in the second half of the 30-day horizon. Apply a non-linear decay factor to the forecast curve, reducing the projected values in days 15-30 to align with the observed tendency for prices to revert to the mean or drop after initial peaks."
  ]
}
